<h2>What Results to Expect</h2>
<p>Light seated pedaling is about 2.0 METs on the Compendium of Physical Activities, compared with about 1.3 for sitting still. For most people that works out to roughly 100 to 200 calories an hour at a light to moderate pace, depending on body weight and resistance. Estimate yours with the <a href="/tools/under-desk-elliptical-calorie-calculator/">calorie calculator</a>.</p>
<p>The bigger win is fewer long, still stretches in your day. Three or four 15 to 20 minute sessions add up to an hour of movement without taking time away from work. Pedaling with real effort also counts toward the CDC and WHO guideline of 150 minutes of moderate activity a week.</p>

<p>Keep the display in perspective too. Its distance and calorie readings are useful for spotting whether you pedaled more this week than last, not for precise tracking. If you log steps on a wrist tracker, it will likely miss seated pedaling, so record your minutes and convert them with our <a href="/tools/under-desk-elliptical-steps-calculator/">steps converter</a> instead.</p>

